A struct declaration introduces a distinct runtime type. Between the pipes,
give each field a name, an explicit type and, if needed, a default.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Vector2 = |
        x F64,
        y F64 = 0.0,
    |
]

Under the planned @../closures/ (closure design), a named struct can also
declare a callback field such as `on_edit |text String| -> EditMessage`.

A named struct has one constructor with the same name as its type. A choice
variant uses the same field list and default rules. See @../choices (Choices).

Use `PascalCase` for the struct name and give each field a unique name.
A field default must evaluate at compile time. The constructor requires an
argument for each field without a default.

Constructors and defaults check values against each field's declared type.
The width alone doesn't determine the type. An `I32` field requires an
`I32`, even though `U32` also occupies 32 bits. Likewise, `Byte` and
`U8` designate distinct types.

### Type identity

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Point = |
        x I32,
        y I32,
    |

    Position = |
        x I32,
        y I32,
    |
]

`Point` and `Position` remain different types despite having identical
fields. A transparent type alias gives a type another name and preserves
its identity.

### Generic structs

Struct declarations may introduce declaration-site generic parameters.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |
]

See @../generics (Generics) for constructor inference, including the rules
for numeric literals in generic fields.
